 ; A fixed expression shape is written as that expression: `[%expr [%e? f] ()]` says "applied to
 ; unit" where the constructors take three lines to say it. Metaquot patterns ignore locations and
 ; attributes but keep arity and labels exact, so they are the whole of the shapes they cover --
 ; the ones they cannot reach (a variable-length argument list, a string constant's value, a module
 ; binding) stay written against the constructors.
 (preprocess
  (pps ppxlib.metaquot ppx_enumerate)))

; The comments and string literals of an OCaml source, read by the compiler's own lexer
; (gh-ocannl-1729): the parse tree holds no plain comment. Through `compiler-libs`, so in a library
; of its own for the reason the emitter frontier's note below gives; its interface is plain data, so
; [test_scan]'s prose readers consume it without seeing either library's `Location`.

(library
 (name prose_lexemes)
 (package neural_nets_lib)
 (modules prose_lexemes)
 (libraries base compiler-libs.common))

; The emitter frontier is derived from the compiler libraries' COMPILED interfaces (gh-ocannl-748),
; so this one reads `.cmi` files through `compiler-libs`. It stays out of [test_scan] because
; that library reads OCaml SOURCES through ppxlib, and the two put different modules under the same
; top-level names (`Parsetree`, `Longident`, `Asttypes`, `Location`): keeping them in separate
; compilation units is what makes each file's spelling unambiguous. Nothing but `base` besides, so
; every consumer of [test_scan] can link it.

(library
 (name emitter_frontier)
 (package neural_nets_lib)
 (modules emitter_frontier)
 (libraries base compiler-libs.common))

